The MIT License below covers the code of this repository: the app's JavaScript,
HTML and CSS, the tools and the tests, and the example data written for it. It does
not cover the content taken from other sources, which keeps its own status:

- The text of the Gregg Shorthand Manual, Anniversary Edition (1929), its figures,
  the brief-form chart, the most-used phrases and the keys to the Reading and
  Dictation Practice (app/data/manual.js, app/data/briefs.js, app/data/reading.js,
  app/manual/) are from J. Andrew Owen's transcription at
  https://greggshorthand.github.io. The 1929 book is in the public domain; his
  edits and transcription are his work, used with credit and links back to his site.
- The images in app/plates/ are cut from scans of the Anniversary Manual (from the
  same site) and of Gregg Speed Studies (1929), both in the public domain.

See "Credits" in README.md.

